/**
 * One retired snapshot of one business record — the storage behind the bitemporal
 * replay required by R-TR-04 / R-TR-05, and the mechanism Demo Reset rewinds through
 * (R-TR-15).
 *
 * Every time a governed field changes, {@link TemporalQueryService#snapshot} freezes
 * the record's pre-change field values into an instance of this type and stamps the
 * outgoing row's {@link Bitemporal#supersededAt}. The live entity always holds the
 * current values; this type holds everything the record used to say. Together they
 * let the UI answer "show me the portfolio exactly as it stood on 12 March" without
 * the live types carrying a version column each.
 *
 * <h3>Why a generic version row rather than per-type history tables</h3>
 * <p>Note on naming: the monotonic counter is {@link EntityVersion#versionNo}, not
 * `version`, because every C3 entity type already inherits a platform `version` field
 * and re-declaring it is a compile error. See that field's documentation.</p>
 * The demo must replay {@link Gate}, {@link Launch}, {@link BatchLot},
 * {@link GateCriterion} and market commitments on the same axis. A single generic
 * snapshot type keyed by (`entityTypeName`, `entityId`, `version`) means the replay
 * service is written once and any newly-governed type gets history for free, at the
 * cost of storing the payload as JSON rather than typed columns — an acceptable
 * trade because these rows are read for display and diffing, never joined on.
 *
 * <h3>Relationship to {@link AuditEvent}</h3>
 * They answer different questions and both are required. An {@link AuditEvent} says
 * *"this field went from X to Y, by whom and why"* — it is the narrative. An
 * `EntityVersion` says *"here is the entire record as it read at that moment"* — it
 * is the state. Reconstructing a screen as-of a date needs whole records, which is
 * why the audit trail alone is not sufficient.
 *
 * Operational type (no `SeedData`): written at runtime by every governed change.
 */
entity type EntityVersion mixes Bitemporal {
  /** Version id — `ver_<entityTypeName>_<entityId>_<versionNo>`. */
  id: !string

  /** C3 type name of the versioned record, e.g. "Gate". */
  entityTypeName: !string

  /** Id of the versioned record, e.g. "seed_gate_bero_p5_g1". */
  entityId: !string

  /**
   * Monotonic version number for this record, starting at 1 for its first snapshot.
   * Ordering by this is stable even when several versions share a millisecond.
   *
   * Named `versionNo` rather than the more natural `version` because every C3 entity type
   * already inherits a `version` field from the platform, and re-declaring it with a
   * different value type is rejected at compile time. The platform's field counts
   * revisions of *one live row*; this one counts snapshots of a record's history, which is
   * a different quantity — so they coexist rather than one replacing the other.
   */
  versionNo: !int

  /**
   * The record's field values at this version, as a JSON object. Read for display
   * and diffing; deliberately schemaless so one type serves every governed entity.
   */
  payload: !json

  /**
   * Dotted field paths that changed *in the revision that retired this snapshot* —
   * i.e. what made it stale. Lets the History tab render "3 fields changed" without
   * diffing two JSON blobs client-side.
   */
  changedFields: [string]

  /**
   * The {@link AuditEvent#correlationId} of the change that retired this snapshot,
   * so a version row can be tied back to the gesture and the audit events that
   * caused it.
   */
  correlationId: string

  /** Who caused the revision that retired this snapshot. Denormalised for display. */
  actorName: string

  /** Coded reason for the revision that retired this snapshot, e.g. "REBASELINE". */
  reasonCode: string
}
